one of the official download pages instead:

- https://www.spigotmc.org/resources/custommotd-custom-textures-proxy-folia-support.139865/
- https://modrinth.com/project/spritemotd
- https://hangar.papermc.io/D4rkSn0w/MOTD

Hosting an unmodified copy of the **source code** publicly, for example as a
fork on a code hosting platform, is permitted provided Section 2 is met. The
restriction above applies to the compiled software, not to the source code.

Publishing a modified version is governed by Sections 2, 3 and 4.

## 2. Attribution Requirements

When distributing this software or a modified version, you must:

- Clearly credit the original author, D4rkSn0w.
- Include a link to the original GitHub repository.
- Preserve all original copyright and license notices.
- Clearly indicate that your project is based on the original MOTD project.
- Clearly describe the modifications and additional features.

Original repository: https://github.com/D4rkSn0w-7652/MOTD

### Where the credit must appear

The credit and the repository link must be visible without reading the source
code, in each of the following places that applies to your distribution:

- On every page from which your version can be downloaded, in the visible
  description text — not only in a changelog, a screenshot, a source comment or
  a collapsed section. This covers resource and project pages on platforms such
  as SpigotMC, Modrinth, Hangar and BuiltByBit as well as your own website.
- In the README or the equivalent front page of your repository, if you publish
  the source code.
- In the LICENSE file or in a NOTICE file shipped alongside your distribution.
- In your version's own plugin information output, if it has one, for example the
  command corresponding to `/motd` in this project.

The description of your modifications required above must be specific enough
that a reader can tell what your version does differently. "Improved",
"optimised" or "fixed" on their own are not sufficient.

## 3. Modification Requirements

Forks and modified versions must introduce meaningful and substantial changes.

The following do not qualify as meaningful modifications on their own:

- Renaming classes, methods or variables.
- Changing package names.
- Changing the plugin name or branding.
- Making minor cosmetic changes.
- Performing superficial code restructuring.

Removing parts of the original also does not qualify on its own. This includes
removing features, the update checker, the metrics collection or the original
branding.

Simply republishing the original project with minor modifications is not permitted.

### What does qualify

A modification qualifies under this Section if it does at least one of the
following, in a way that is apparent to someone using the plugin:

- Adds a feature the original does not have.
- Adds support for a platform, a server version or an API the original does not support.
- Changes how an existing feature behaves, in a way that is configurable or
  visible in the result.
- Connects the plugin to another plugin or to an external system.
- Replaces or substantially reworks an existing implementation for a stated
  technical reason, such as performance, compatibility or memory use.

Translations, changed config defaults, reformatting and build configuration
changes are welcome, but do not by themselves meet this requirement.

Whether a modification meets this requirement is determined by the copyright
holder. If you are unsure, ask before publishing — asking is free and an answer
in advance is binding: https://discord.gg/AccQuwNYfT

## 4. Distribution Restrictions

You are not permitted to:

- Sell this software or modified versions.
- Offer this software or modified versions as paid downloads.
- Place this software or modified versions behind a paywall, a purchase, a
  subscription or any other payment, including as part of a paid bundle.
- Redistribute the original project as your own.
- Offer the unmodified compiled software as a public download, as set out in Section 1.
- Publish modified versions without complying with the attribution and modification requirements.
- Remove or alter original copyright and license notices.
- Claim original authorship or ownership of the original project.

For the avoidance of doubt: running this software on a server or network that
earns money is not a violation of this Section, as stated in Section 1.

